&lt;p&gt;Columbia Center for Community Health and&amp;nbsp;Plant Powered Metro New York are hosting a free community screening of&amp;nbsp;&lt;em&gt;&lt;em&gt;Eating You Alive&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&amp;nbsp;in West Harlem.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Whether you&amp;rsquo;re passionate about improving your own health or supporting the health of your community,&amp;nbsp;&lt;em&gt;&lt;em&gt;Eating You Alive&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&amp;nbsp;offers an inspiring look at how food can play a powerful role in preventing, treating, and even reversing chronic disease.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Half of all adults in the U.S. struggle with chronic health conditions including cancer, heart disease, stroke, diabetes, obesity, hypertension, Alzheimer&amp;rsquo;s, arthritis and erectile dysfunction.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Despite countless dollars spent on medical research, new drugs and innovative technology to improve our health, more Americans are disabled and dying from chronic disease than ever before.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Featuring leading medical experts and researchers,&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;Eating You Alive&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;takes a scientific look at the reasons we&amp;rsquo;re so sick, who&amp;rsquo;s responsible for feeding us the wrong information and how we can use whole-food,&amp;nbsp;plant-based nutrition to take control of our health&amp;mdash;one bite at a time.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Stay after the screening for a community conversation with special guests where we&amp;#39;ll explore key takeaways, answer questions, and share ideas for bringing the messages and ideas from the film into our homes and neighborhoods.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Join us for the screening of the thought-provoking documentary, Eating You Alive, followed by a community discussion.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Columbia Center for Community Health and Plant Powered Metro New York are hosting a free community screening of&amp;nbsp;&lt;em&gt;&lt;em&gt;Eating You Alive&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&amp;nbsp;in West Harlem.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Whether you&amp;rsquo;re passionate about improving your own health or supporting the health of your community,&amp;nbsp;&lt;em&gt;&lt;em&gt;Eating You Alive&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&amp;nbsp;offers an inspiring look at how food can play a powerful role in preventing, treating, and even reversing chronic disease. Featuring leading physicians, researchers, and personal stories, the film explores the science behind whole food, plant-based nutrition and the profound impact it can have on our health.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Stay after the screening for a community conversation with special guests where we&amp;#39;ll explore key takeaways, answer questions, and share ideas for bringing the messages and ideas from the film into our homes and neighborhoods.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Join us on August 28 at 8:00pm in Pupin 301 for a talk by postdoc Dr. Rebecca Diesing about uncovering the Universe&amp;#39;s most extreme events.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
Title: Reading the Shrapnel of Cosmic Fireworks&lt;br /&gt;
Abstract: &amp;ldquo;The Universe is full of cosmic fireworks: stars explode, clusters of galaxies collide, and black holes blow winds reaching a hundred million miles per hour. These explosions, collisions, and outbursts produce shock waves that accelerate particles like shrapnel to nearly the speed of light, and those particles in turn produce light that travels across the Universe to us. By studying this light&amp;mdash;shrapnel of shrapnel&amp;mdash;we can uncover the physics that powers some of the Universe&amp;rsquo;s most extreme events.&amp;rdquo;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
We&amp;#39;ll start the evening at 8:00pm with some astronomy trivia, followed by the talk and Q&amp;amp;A. From 9:10-10:00pm, we will be outside observing on campus (weather permitting).&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
If you do not have a CUID or would like to bring someone who doesn&amp;rsquo;t have one, please follow the instructions and register here: https://forms.gle/ESL8C8pYH6uJVAoP9 by August 26 midnight. Check out more about our events and join our mailing list on our website! https://outreach.astro.columbia.edu/&amp;nbsp;&lt;br /&gt;
&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Please join us for the NYC Voices Community Film Festival!&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Presented by Columbia University Mailman School of Public Health&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;FORWARD Community Engagement Action Corps,&lt;/strong&gt; the&lt;strong&gt; Office of Inclusive Excellence (OIE)&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;and the&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;Community Health Collaborative (CHEC)&lt;/strong&gt;,&amp;nbsp;The&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;NYC Voices Community&amp;nbsp;Film Festival&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;aims to encourage creativity in communicating about&amp;nbsp;public health issues&amp;nbsp;that are important to our local New York City communities through storytelling, digital media, and the belief that&amp;nbsp;we all have a story to tell!&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Please join us for an evening of&amp;nbsp;storytelling&amp;nbsp;as we celebrate&amp;nbsp;New York City voices&amp;nbsp;through a selection of&amp;nbsp;films&amp;nbsp;created&amp;nbsp;and&amp;nbsp;submitted&amp;nbsp;by community members, as well as a&amp;nbsp;feature documentary presentation and Q&amp;amp;A.&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
